04:21 iclr2026_conference.sty\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 04:30 literature\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   11450 Nov  7 04:51 memory.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   16899 Nov  7 04:21 official_template.tex\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   13083 Nov  7 04:50 paper.aux\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    7802 Nov  7 04:50 paper.bbl\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     933 Nov  7 04:50 paper.blg\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   26973 Nov  2 14:28 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   16843 Nov  7 04:50 paper.fls\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   21633 Nov  7 04:50 paper.log\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    4740 Nov  7 04:50 paper.out\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent  284227 Nov  7 04:50 paper.pdf\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     568 Nov  7 04:51 paper.pdf.prov.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    8934 Nov  2 14:28 paper.sty\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   51428 Nov  7 04:47 paper.tex\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     390 Nov  7 04:51 paper.tex.prov.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent 1998213 Nov  7 04:52 paper_agent_codex_20261107_040258.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent 9047640 Nov  7 04:03 paper_data.json\ndrwx------  3 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 04:08 paper_diagnostics\n-rw-------  1 agent agent       0 Nov  7 04:02 paper_diagnostics_20261107_040258_42062.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   11859 Nov  7 04:52 paper_events_20261107_040258.jsonl\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     791 Nov  7 04:02 paper_prep_20261107_040258.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   72461 Nov  7 04:18 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   29013 Nov  7 04:17 paper_strategy.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent   20934 Nov  7 04:17 paper_strategy.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent  490736 Nov  7 04:18 paper_strategy_agent_codex_20261107_040258.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   46258 Nov  7 04:10 paper_strategy_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     331 Nov  7 04:18 paper_strategy_resolution.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    8111 Nov  7 04:08 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    8721 Nov  7 04:08 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent 1257671 Nov  7 04:08 reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261107_040258.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   30267 Nov  7 04:03 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     265 Nov  7 04:08 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     279 Nov  7 04:08 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    3245 Nov  7 04:10 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    7101 Nov  7 04:45 references.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   13628 Nov  7 04:45 references_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      61 Nov  7 04:44 requirements-evaluated.txt\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      97 Nov  7 04:44 requirements-paper.txt\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent      16 Nov  7 04:02 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 04:51 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 04:44 scripts\ndrwx------  3 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 04:42 source_evidence\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     265 Nov  7 04:18 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     271 Nov  7 04:18 strategy_validation_feedback.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent    4096 Nov  7 04:25 tables\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent       7 Nov  7 04:02 task -> ../task\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    1068 Nov  7 04:51 template_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent    1012 Nov  7 04:51 validation_report.json\n    420 paper.tex\n    224 references.bib\n    279 references_provenance.json\n   2060 claims_grounding.json\n 272450 paper_data.json\n    205 paper_strategy.json\n     48 global_memory_summary.md\n 275686 total\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,longtable,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion}\n\\newcommand{\\budget}{B}\n\\newcommand{\\tol}{\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nMultitask leaderboards aggregate heterogeneous scores, creating incentives to improve selected benchmark tasks rather than broad capability. How much targeted investment is needed to reach the top, and does the answer depend on the aggregation rule? We study a static score-space model: investment selects tasks, each selected score receives a bounded fraction of its remaining headroom, and competitors remain fixed. We compare arithmetic mean, ordinary median, and pairwise Borda aggregation on public HELM MMLU and Open LLM score snapshots. Independently checked allocation bounds reveal cap-dependent reversals: for one HELM target, mean versus median budgets change from 9 versus 12 tasks at full headroom to 23 versus 12 at half headroom; an Open LLM target exhibits the same qualitative reversal. Some promotions become unattainable under the smaller cap. These results reject a cap-independent resistance ordering on the evaluated instances. They quantify conditional score-space vulnerability, not the training data, compute, or money needed to realize such changes.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations]]\n% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nPublic multitask benchmarks compress a model's performance profile into a leaderboard position. MMLU and BIG-Bench Hard exemplify evaluations with heterogeneous component tasks, while HELM emphasizes transparency and breadth of evaluation \\citep{Hendrycks2020MMLU,Suzgun2022BBH,Liang2022HELM}. Once adoption and recognition depend on position, a developer may have an incentive to concentrate resources on a subset of benchmark tasks. A leaderboard's aggregation rule determines which score changes are rewarded. Evaluating that incentive requires specifying both the allowed changes and the meaning of investment.\n\nWe ask a deliberately conditional question: on a fixed public score table, what is the smallest task subset whose improvement would make a target model co-first? Investment is the number of selected tasks, not the amount of training data. Each selected coordinate receives a stipulated fraction of its remaining score headroom; every unselected coordinate and every competitor's coordinate stays fixed. This model separates a leaderboard's response to score changes from the unresolved question of how reliably training can produce those changes. It is an analysis of a possible response envelope, not evidence that a developer can realize the envelope.\n\nA common intuition is that the median should resist concentrated improvements because isolated extreme scores need not affect its value. That intuition does not settle the relative cost of reaching the top. A mean rewards every increment, whereas a median depends on crossing order-statistic thresholds. Reducing the permitted increment can change which threshold crossings remain available without proportionally changing the median's task budget. Conversely, a low starting median can require many changes even when a mean responds cheaply. The relevant object is therefore a budget conditioned jointly on the target, aggregation rule, and response cap.\n\nOur study makes three concrete contributions. It defines a common bounded intervention model for mean, median, and Borda; expresses scalar allocation through sorting or threshold certificates and Borda through rival-aware integer constraints; and reports paired budgets, unattainability, and matched comparator results on frozen public score tables. The central finding is not a universally preferable aggregation rule. The same-target mean--median ordering reverses when the cap changes in both evaluated settings. Reference bounds and explicit failure states make that conclusion auditable without interpreting a timeout as impossibility.\n\nThe study is intentionally narrower than a strategic training equilibrium. Competitors do not react, task costs are uniform, and no model is trained. These restrictions preserve an interpretable comparison but preclude claims about real resource expenditure, general capability, or a population-wide frequency of reversal. The full target panel, including targets already co-first under a different rule, is retained rather than filtering to favorable examples.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/score_space_overview.png}\n\\caption{The score-space intervention model. A fixed public score table and target define the comparison. Selecting tasks raises only the target's selected coordinates to a specified headroom cap. Mean, ordinary median, and rival-aware Borda yield a minimum task budget or certified unattainability. The cap describes a hypothetical response, not a measured training effect.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Evaluation design and incentives.}\nThe benchmark lottery shows that task selection can alter comparative conclusions \\citep{Dehghani2021Lottery}. Here the task set stays fixed while the aggregation rule and permitted response change. HELM's multidimensional evaluation and the language-model evaluation harness motivate explicit task definitions and reproducible score processing \\citep{Liang2022HELM,Gao2023Harness}; our computations use released scores rather than rerunning either evaluation system. Analyses of leaderboard incentives and selective evaluation provide complementary reasons not to equate rank with general capability \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}. Strategic post-training has also been modeled as an interaction between a benchmark designer and competing developers \\citep{Chen2026Strategic}. We instead hold rivals fixed and measure discrete promotion budgets, without estimating an equilibrium or latent quality.\n\n\\paragraph{Social choice and targeted changes.}\nTreating tasks as voters connects model evaluation with social choice \\citep{Lanctot2023SocialChoice,Brandt2016Handbook}; aggregating rankings is also a classical information-retrieval problem \\citep{Dwork2001Rank}. Strategic preference reporting \\citep{Gibbard1973Manipulation} and election bribery \\citep{Faliszewski2009Bribery} establish useful distinctions between aggregation and manipulation. Our intervention changes only a target's numerical task coordinates, according to a fixed response cap. It cannot arbitrarily rewrite rankings or remove competitors. In particular, a Borda calculation must update rivals as the target overtakes them, rather than compare against frozen Borda totals.\n\n\\paragraph{Robustness and contamination.}\nClassical robust estimation distinguishes local influence from resistance under a specified contamination model \\citep{Huber1964Robust,Hampel1974Influence,Rousseeuw1984Median}. These distinctions caution against importing a slogan about median robustness into an adaptive, bounded, top-rank objective. Our coordinates are heterogeneous tasks, not independent observations of a common location parameter. Benchmark leakage and rephrased contamination can affect measured model performance \\citep{Zhou2023Cheater,Yang2023Rephrased}, but those findings do not calibrate our headroom response. We neither identify contaminated models nor infer that task-specific data necessarily produces the score increments analyzed here. The contribution is an instance-level comparison under stated assumptions, not a claim of first priority over all work on strategic evaluation.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and intervention model}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times d}$ contain higher-is-better task scores, with target row $t$ and original target vector $x=X_t$. For a selected task set $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ and cap $\\rho$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n u_j(\\rho)=\\operatorname{round}_{10}\\!\\left(x_j+\\rho(1-x_j)\\right),\\qquad\n y_j(S,\\rho)=\\begin{cases}u_j(\\rho),&j\\in S,\\\\x_j,&j\\notin S.\\end{cases}\n \\label{eq:response}\n\\end{equation}\nThe evaluated positive caps are $\\rho=1$ and $\\rho=1/2$. Input scores and endpoints are rounded to ten decimal places. A separate no-update control uses zero response. Task selection is the decision variable; the cap is an externally specified condition, not a fitted learning curve. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes this distinction.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L260]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nWrite $A_r(i;X)$ for row $i$'s aggregate under rule $r$, and $X^{t\\leftarrow y}$ for the modified table. Promotion means co-first within tolerance $\\tol=10^{-10}$:\n\\begin{equation}\n A_r(t;X^{t\\leftarrow y})\\geq \\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(i;X^{t\\leftarrow y})-\\tol.\n \\label{eq:success}\n\\end{equation}\nWe minimize the uniform investment\n\\begin{equation}\n \\budget_r(t,\\rho)=\\min_S\\{|S|:\\text{Equation~\\eqref{eq:success} holds}\\},\n \\qquad b_r(t,\\rho)=\\budget_r(t,\\rho)/d.\n \\label{eq:objective}\n\\end{equation}\nIf no subset succeeds, $\\budget_r=\\infty$. An empty successful subset has budget zero; an empty response reporting unattainability does not. Co-first is weaker than strict victory, and the results do not address arbitrary tie-breaking policies.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L1]]\n\n\\subsection{Design rationale and compared approaches}\nWe compare arithmetic mean, ordinary median, and pairwise Borda on identical targets and endpoints. The even median averages the two central order statistics. Borda awards one point for a task-level pairwise win and half a point for a tie, then averages across opponents and tasks:\n\\begin{equation}\n A_{\\mathrm{B}}(i;X)=\\frac{1}{d(n-1)}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\sum_{h\\ne i}\n \\left[\\mathbf{1}\\{X_{ij}>X_{hj}\\}+\\tfrac12\\mathbf{1}\\{X_{ij}=X_{hj}\\}\\right].\n \\label{eq:borda}\n\\end{equation}\nChanging the target can lower a rival's Borda total even though the rival's underlying task scores are unchanged.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L50]]\n\nThe hypothesis is conditional: the ordering of task budgets need not survive a change in cap. This follows mechanistically from distinct sufficient statistics. Mean uses total added score mass; median uses central threshold crossings; Borda uses pairwise crossings against the whole competitor population. None of these mechanisms implies the same ranking of investment costs on every target vector.\n\nThere is nevertheless a simple within-rule restriction. Holding the table and target fixed, a larger cap weakly expands the set of successful subsets. Thus, with infinity ordered above finite budgets,\n\\begin{equation}\n \\rho_1\\leq\\rho_2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n \\budget_r(t,\\rho_1)\\geq\\budget_r(t,\\rho_2).\n \\label{eq:monotone}\n\\end{equation}\nThis implication follows from the same target--rival monotonicity used by the endpoint test. It does not order different rules: their budgets may increase by different amounts when the cap shrinks. Observing a reversal between rules therefore does not contradict monotonicity of either rule's own budget. This separation distinguishes the proposed comparison from an assertion that a smaller permitted improvement can make promotion cheaper.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L260]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L193]]\n\nAllocation methods are compared with no intervention, marginal greedy selection, and a seeded random task order evaluated by successive prefixes. Marginal greedy chooses the task producing the largest current improvement in the target's gap to its strongest rival, with deterministic task-order ties. A random order is drawn for each decision, not optimized over many orders. These are selection policies under the same response model, not alternative training procedures. The original development comparator uses exhaustive allocation on small tables and greedy allocation otherwise; its selection score does not supply a matched final population comparison.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py#L59]]\n% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/baselines]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study]]\n\n\\subsection{Scalar allocation certificates}\nFor mean and median, competitors' aggregates stay fixed. Let $c_r=\\max_{i\\ne t}A_r(i;X)$. Raising selected coordinates to their endpoints cannot reduce any studied aggregate or worsen any target--rival gap. Thus the all-task endpoint vector certifies reachability: if it cannot win, no subset can. Conversely, because selected tasks have unit cost and no penalty for a larger allowed response, endpoint updates suffice for the stated optimization problem.\n\nFor the mean, let $g_j=u_j-x_j$, and sort gains decreasingly as $g_{(1)}\\geq\\cdots\\geq g_{(d)}$. The minimum finite budget is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n\\begin{equation}\n \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k} g_{(\\ell)}\\geq d\\bigl(c_{\\mathrm{mean}}-\\tol-\\bar{x}\\bigr).\n \\label{eq:mean}\n\\end{equation}\nNo other $k$ tasks can supply more gain, giving a cardinality certificate rather than just a successful witness.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L73]]\n\nFor odd $d=2m+1$, median success requires at least $m+1$ coordinates at or above $c_{\\mathrm{median}}-\\tol$. Count those already above that threshold, then count coordinates whose endpoint crosses it. The required number of crossings gives the minimum if enough are available. For even $d=2m$, success depends on the sum of the two central values, not a single threshold. The solver enumerates candidate lower central endpoints and the smallest compatible upper endpoint. It checks the nested requirements of at least $m+1$ values above the lower endpoint and at least $m$ above the upper endpoint, sharing upgrades where possible. Final aggregate checks protect the numerical boundary. The small even-dimensional setting also uses exhaustive subset verification. Appendix~\\ref{sec:scalar} gives the counting construction.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L82]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L93]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L129]]\n\n\\subsection{Rival-aware Borda allocation}\nUse twice-credit integer totals $T_i=2d(n-1)A_{\\mathrm{B}}(i;X)$. Let $\\delta_{ij}\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ be the increase in the target's twice-credit against rival $i$ on task $j$, and $G_j=\\sum_{i\\ne t}\\delta_{ij}$. Selecting task $j$ increases the target's total by $G_j$ and decreases rival $i$'s total by $\\delta_{ij}$. With binary task indicators $z_j$, the necessary and sufficient constraints are\n\\begin{equation}\n \\sum_j (G_j+\\delta_{ij})z_j\\geq D_i,\n \\quad D_i=\\left\\lceil T_i-T_t-2d(n-1)\\tol\\right\\rceil,\n \\quad i\\ne t.\n \\label{eq:cover}\n\\end{equation}\nOnly initially positive deficits constrain the solution, since all gap improvements are nonnegative. This formulation accounts for changing rival totals explicitly.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L193]]\n\nThe supplied selected source builds a feasible gain-ordered prefix, deletes unnecessary tasks, and searches smaller cardinalities using individual-rival bounds and suffix pruning. Search is bounded by a deadline and a node limit; on interruption it returns a verified incumbent, not an optimality assertion. In the recorded original instances, independent reference bounds close and match every finite returned cardinality. That observed agreement is stronger than a feasible-witness check, but it does not establish exactness for arbitrary larger instances or superiority of a particular search component. No controlled component ablation is available.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L160]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\n\\section{Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol}\n\\paragraph{Snapshots and preprocessing.}\nThe study uses HELM's MMLU release v1.13.0 and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot, acquired on 7 November 2026. HELM contributes 57 subject scores; Open LLM contributes six normalized leaderboard columns: IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-PRO. Open LLM scores are divided by 100; its additional raw columns are not treated as independent tasks. The comparison recomputes all three aggregation rules from these component scores. It is not a claim about the live ordering displayed by either website.\n% @evidence[[data/helm.json#/tasks]]\n% @evidence[[data/openllm.json#/tasks]]\n% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/normalization]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nScore content and known model aliases are clustered before a deterministic content-based split separates development from final analysis. Exact duplicates are collapsed; normalization is applied after assignment. The frozen final competitor tables, not just the selected targets, determine every comparison. Known alias matching reduces exact overlap but cannot establish unseen-model-family independence because lineage information is incomplete. Appendix~\\ref{sec:repro} specifies versions, hashes, and the acquisition code.\n\n\\paragraph{Targets and matched comparisons.}\nEach final score table has eight fixed targets, selected at rank-spaced positions among nonwinners under its original arithmetic mean. Target selection is not repeated separately for each rule. Consequently, a target can already be co-first under median or Borda. We retain such zero-budget cases. Each target is assessed under every rule at both positive caps, keeping all other inputs fixed. Target labels are frozen row indices, not model-family categories.\n% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/target_selection]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/targets]]\n\nThe records contain three seeded executions, with seeds 42011, 42013, and 42017, of the same target instances. There are 48 distinct target--rule--cap decisions per score table. Independent validation recomputes aggregate success and checks the reference bounds. The reference uses exhaustive subsets for the six-task table, sorting or threshold counting for scalar rules on HELM, and a time-limited binary optimization reference for HELM Borda. We report a finite optimum only where a valid witness and matching lower and upper bounds are recorded; unresolved optimization would remain a bound interval. Independently certified unattainability is kept separate from missing or failed computation.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/baselines]]\n\n\\paragraph{Checks and uncertainty.}\nTask permutations, constant-score tables, and zero-update controls test implementation behavior and are excluded from the empirical target denominator. A recorded reset replay checks a repeated status/effort signature. Repeated seeds do not resample tasks or models. We therefore report deterministic paired differences and observed repeatability, not confidence intervals for an unspecified population. The random comparator's range reflects only its recorded orders. No statistical test is used to turn these fixed-panel counterexamples into a universal rule ranking.\n\n\\section{Results: Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets}\n\\subsection{Within-target reversals}\nTable~\\ref{tab:budgets} reports every original target. Figure~\\ref{fig:paired} places the mean and median budgets on common normalized axes. Points above the diagonal require more tasks under median; points below it require more under mean. The shaded rails retain unattainable cases rather than assigning them an artificial large finite budget. Multiplicities are explicit, and seeded repeats are not plotted as independent targets.\n\nFor HELM target 10, the mean/median budgets are 9/12 tasks at full headroom and 23/12 at half headroom. For Open LLM target 1609, they are 2/3 and 4/3, respectively. Thus the direction of the same-target comparison reverses in both score tables. These examples refute a cap-independent ordering on these instances; they do not estimate how frequently such reversals occur among future models.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/4/observations]]\n\nThe reversal is interpretable without attributing it to training. Smaller increments reduce the total gain available to the mean, requiring additional coordinates. The median can retain its task count when the necessary endpoint threshold crossings remain possible. HELM targets 37, 18, and 28 exhibit unchanged median counts across the two caps while their mean counts increase. This is a property of their observed score geometry under the stipulated response, not a claim that median is insensitive to cap changes in general. HELM target 40 already has median budget zero at both caps despite being a mean-selected nonwinner.\n% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\caption{Minimum selected-task counts on all original targets. $\\infty$ means unattainable under the stated cap. Every finite count matches a closed independent reference bound in each recorded seeded execution. Task counts are within-snapshot investments, not calibrated training costs.}\n\\label{tab:budgets}\n\\input{tables/budgets.tex}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\begin{subfigure}{\\textwidth}\n\\centering\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/paired_mean_median_budgets.pdf}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\caption{Paired minimum task fractions for identical targets. Top: HELM; bottom: Open LLM. Left: full headroom; right: half headroom. NA rails indicate certified unattainability, not missing values or an additional numeric range. Annotated row indices identify targets that reverse their mean--median ordering. Labels $\\times k$ denote coincident target points. All finite points have matching independent lower and upper cardinality bounds.}\n\\label{fig:paired}\n% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Reachability and Borda}\nAt full headroom, all eight targets in each table are reachable under all three rules. This is expected structurally: selecting every coordinate sets the target to the scale maximum. The informative full-headroom quantity is therefore the minimum task budget, not reachability alone. At half headroom, HELM has five reachable targets under mean, five under median, and four under Borda; Open LLM has seven, six, and three. Figure~\\ref{fig:reachability} uses the same target denominator throughout. An all-task cap that fails the rank condition certifies these unattainable cases. Such a result is a limit of the intervention model, not evidence of an unsuccessful training run or a solver timeout.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/reachability]]\n\nBorda can require a large selected subset because improving a task matters through the rival crossings it enables. For Open LLM target 1566, full-headroom Borda requires all six tasks, but half-headroom Borda is unattainable; mean and median remain reachable with four tasks at half headroom. HELM target 28 is also unreachable under half-headroom Borda while mean and median remain reachable. These are comparisons between precisely defined rule-and-cap configurations. They do not isolate a causal benefit of ordinal aggregation, since the aggregation objective itself changes.\n% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\begin{subfigure}{\\textwidth}\n\\centering\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/cap_conditioned_reachability.pdf}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\caption{Reachable original targets out of eight, by aggregation rule and response cap. The denominator excludes seeded repeats and synthetic controls. Reachability means co-first under the cap, not general model improvement. All full-headroom cases are reachable; half-headroom failures differ by rule and score table.}\n\\label{fig:reachability}\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/reachability]]\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Allocation checks and comparator interpretation}\nAcross the original decisions, all recorded reference intervals close. Every finite selected cardinality equals its reference minimum, and every reported unattainable decision agrees with the independent reference. The selected task subsets are identical across the three seeds, not merely equal in size. These observations support the precision of the reported finite-instance comparisons, not a claim about unrestricted solver completeness.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\nMarginal greedy matches the minimum on the recorded feasible mean and Borda decisions. It can miss median budgets because immediate marginal changes do not necessarily reward the threshold crossings needed for eventual promotion. For HELM target 28 at half headroom, the certified median minimum is 21 tasks, compared with 52 for the recorded greedy policy. This gap concerns a selection policy on that instance; no ablation identifies which internal solver component accounts for it. Nor is it a general advantage over the original development comparator: the frozen selection had no aggregate development-score improvement over that comparator. Matched random-order costs and all selected subsets are supplied in the supplement and machine-readable records.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/comparators]]\n% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study/search_score_delta]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{What the investment measures.}\nA task count measures concentration of intervention support. It does not measure examples, tokens, training steps, compute, or money. The response in Equation~\\eqref{eq:response} is hypothetical and favorable: all selected improvements are jointly available, improvements do not spill into unselected tasks, and no forgetting occurs. Real training can violate every condition. A smaller task budget can therefore coexist with a larger real training cost. The empirical conclusions apply only to the stated score response, uniform task costs, and fixed competitors.\n\n\\paragraph{Why rule robustness is conditional.}\nMedian robustness in a location-estimation problem does not by itself determine the cost of overtaking a particular competitor. Here the objective is relative rank, and the target's starting position matters. Borda additionally depends on the whole competitor population; mean and median depend on their respective strongest rival aggregate. A change in the competitor set or task composition could change the comparisons. The analysis holds those objects fixed, rather than selecting a favorable population for each rule. Normalizing by task count enables a visual comparison but does not make selecting a subject equivalent in practical effort to selecting an entire benchmark.\n\n\\paragraph{Coverage and uncertainty.}\nThe paired design identifies cap sensitivity on the observed instances, not how common the phenomenon is beyond them. Targets are mean-rank stratified rather than a random model sample. The two score snapshots share an evaluation ecosystem and can include related model lineages. Hash and alias separation does not establish independence of model development or broad transfer. Only two positive caps were evaluated; the figures are discrete comparisons, not continuous response frontiers. No composition resampling, strict-victory sensitivity, heterogeneous-cost study, or empirical training-response calibration is available. These omissions constrain generalization but do not invalidate the displayed within-target counterexamples.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\n\\paragraph{Computation and scientific scope.}\nA feasible witness alone would not certify a minimum; a time limit alone would not certify impossibility. We preserve both distinctions. Bound closure happens to support exact cardinalities on the recorded original instances, although Borda search is bounded in the implementation. The separate final assessment is completed and qualified; that status neither establishes novelty nor enlarges the scope of the scientific conclusions. The development comparison has no positive aggregate difference, and no component ablation supports a claim of optimizer superiority. Public availability of score files also does not settle their redistribution rights; the reproduction instructions preserve upstream provenance and describe reacquisition rather than assuming a code or model license covers score data.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study/search_score_delta]]\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nTargeted leaderboard promotion has no meaningful task budget until both the aggregation rule and the allowed score response are specified. Under a bounded headroom model on two frozen public score tables, paired minimum budgets reverse their mean--median ordering for some targets when the cap is reduced. Reachability also changes by rule, including cases where using every task is insufficient. The evidence supports these finite-instance, conditional conclusions rather than a universal ranking of aggregation rules.\n\nThe practical implication is methodological: report the target score vector, response assumptions, competitor population, success criterion, and allocation bounds together. A single aggregate vulnerability score hides distinctions between inexpensive promotion, already being co-first, and promotion that is impossible under the cap. Extending this analysis to real resource allocation requires evidence linking training investments to joint score changes and their side effects. Until such calibration is available, task-count budgets are interpretable counterfactual diagnostics, not estimates of the cost of producing a better model.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Endpoint reduction and scalar certificates}\\label{sec:scalar}\nFor each rule, increasing only a target coordinate weakly improves every target--rival gap. For mean and median this follows from coordinatewise monotonicity with unchanged rival aggregates. For Borda, the target gains pairwise credit while any overtaken rival loses the corresponding credit. Consequently, if the all-task endpoint vector is unsuccessful, every subset is unsuccessful. If a selected task could instead receive any intermediate increase below its endpoint at the same unit cost, replacing it by its endpoint cannot destroy success. This endpoint reduction does not apply when improvements incur magnitude-dependent costs, adversely affect other coordinates, or provoke responding rivals.\n\nFor mean, the largest-gain prefix maximizes added score over subsets of a fixed size, proving Equation~\\eqref{eq:mean}. For odd median, put $h=c_{\\mathrm{median}}-\\tol$, $a=\\#\\{j:x_j\\geq h\\}$, and $p=\\#\\{j:x_j<h\\leq u_j\\}$. With $d=2m+1$, the finite minimum is $k=\\max(0,m+1-a)$ if $k\\leq p$, and is otherwise unattainable. This is a threshold count, not a local derivative of the median.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L73]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,175p'; cat references.bib; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,175p'; cat references.bib; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n     3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n     4\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n     5\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,longtable,subcaption}\n     6\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n     7\t\\title{Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion}\n     8\t\\newcommand{\\budget}{B}\n     9\t\\newcommand{\\tol}{\\varepsilon}\n    10\t\\begin{document}\n    11\t\\maketitle\n    12\t\n    13\t\\begin{abstract}\n    14\tMultitask leaderboards aggregate heterogeneous scores, creating incentives to improve selected benchmark tasks rather than broad capability. How much targeted investment is needed to reach the top, and does the answer depend on the aggregation rule? We study a static score-space model: investment selects tasks, each selected score receives a bounded fraction of its remaining headroom, and competitors remain fixed. We compare arithmetic mean, ordinary median, and pairwise Borda aggregation on public HELM MMLU and Open LLM score snapshots. Independently checked allocation bounds reveal cap-dependent reversals: for one HELM target, mean versus median budgets change from 9 versus 12 tasks at full headroom to 23 versus 12 at half headroom; an Open LLM target exhibits the same qualitative reversal. Some promotions become unattainable under the smaller cap. These results reject a cap-independent resistance ordering on the evaluated instances. They quantify conditional score-space vulnerability, not the training data, compute, or money needed to realize such changes.\n    15\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations]]\n    16\t% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n    17\t\\end{abstract}\n    18\t\n    19\t\\section{Introduction}\n    20\tPublic multitask benchmarks compress a model's performance profile into a leaderboard position. MMLU and BIG-Bench Hard exemplify evaluations with heterogeneous component tasks, while HELM emphasizes transparency and breadth of evaluation \\citep{Hendrycks2020MMLU,Suzgun2022BBH,Liang2022HELM}. Once adoption and recognition depend on position, a developer may have an incentive to concentrate resources on a subset of benchmark tasks. A leaderboard's aggregation rule determines which score changes are rewarded. Evaluating that incentive requires specifying both the allowed changes and the meaning of investment.\n    21\t\n    22\tWe ask a deliberately conditional question: on a fixed public score table, what is the smallest task subset whose improvement would make a target model co-first? Investment is the number of selected tasks, not the amount of training data. Each selected coordinate receives a stipulated fraction of its remaining score headroom; every unselected coordinate and every competitor's coordinate stays fixed. This model separates a leaderboard's response to score changes from the unresolved question of how reliably training can produce those changes. It is an analysis of a possible response envelope, not evidence that a developer can realize the envelope.\n    23\t\n    24\tA common intuition is that the median should resist concentrated improvements because isolated extreme scores need not affect its value. That intuition does not settle the relative cost of reaching the top. A mean rewards every increment, whereas a median depends on crossing order-statistic thresholds. Reducing the permitted increment can change which threshold crossings remain available without proportionally changing the median's task budget. Conversely, a low starting median can require many changes even when a mean responds cheaply. The relevant object is therefore a budget conditioned jointly on the target, aggregation rule, and response cap.\n    25\t\n    26\tOur study makes three concrete contributions. It defines a common bounded intervention model for mean, median, and Borda; expresses scalar allocation through sorting or threshold certificates and Borda through rival-aware integer constraints; and reports paired budgets, unattainability, and matched comparator results on frozen public score tables. The central finding is not a universally preferable aggregation rule. The same-target mean--median ordering reverses when the cap changes in both evaluated settings. Reference bounds and explicit failure states make that conclusion auditable without interpreting a timeout as impossibility.\n    27\t\n    28\tThe study is intentionally narrower than a strategic training equilibrium. Competitors do not react, task costs are uniform, and no model is trained. These restrictions preserve an interpretable comparison but preclude claims about real resource expenditure, general capability, or a population-wide frequency of reversal. The full target panel, including targets already co-first under a different rule, is retained rather than filtering to favorable examples.\n    29\t\n    30\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    31\t\\centering\n    32\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/score_space_overview.png}\n    33\t\\caption{The score-space intervention model. A fixed public score table and target define the comparison. Selecting tasks raises only the target's selected coordinates to a specified headroom cap. Mean, ordinary median, and rival-aware Borda yield a minimum task budget or certified unattainability. The cap describes a hypothetical response, not a measured training effect.}\n    34\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    35\t\\end{figure}\n    36\t\n    37\t\\section{Related Work}\n    38\t\\paragraph{Evaluation design and incentives.}\n    39\tThe benchmark lottery shows that task selection can alter comparative conclusions \\citep{Dehghani2021Lottery}. Here the task set stays fixed while the aggregation rule and permitted response change. HELM's multidimensional evaluation and the language-model evaluation harness motivate explicit task definitions and reproducible score processing \\citep{Liang2022HELM,Gao2023Harness}; our computations use released scores rather than rerunning either evaluation system. Analyses of leaderboard incentives and selective evaluation provide complementary reasons not to equate rank with general capability \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}. Strategic post-training has also been modeled as an interaction between a benchmark designer and competing developers \\citep{Chen2026Strategic}. We instead hold rivals fixed and measure discrete promotion budgets, without estimating an equilibrium or latent quality.\n    40\t\n    41\t\\paragraph{Social choice and targeted changes.}\n    42\tTreating tasks as voters connects model evaluation with social choice \\citep{Lanctot2023SocialChoice,Brandt2016Handbook}; aggregating rankings is also a classical information-retrieval problem \\citep{Dwork2001Rank}. Strategic preference reporting \\citep{Gibbard1973Manipulation} and election bribery \\citep{Faliszewski2009Bribery} establish useful distinctions between aggregation and manipulation. Our intervention changes only a target's numerical task coordinates, according to a fixed response cap. It cannot arbitrarily rewrite rankings or remove competitors. In particular, a Borda calculation must update rivals as the target overtakes them, rather than compare against frozen Borda totals.\n    43\t\n    44\t\\paragraph{Robustness and contamination.}\n    45\tClassical robust estimation distinguishes local influence from resistance under a specified contamination model \\citep{Huber1964Robust,Hampel1974Influence,Rousseeuw1984Median}. These distinctions caution against importing a slogan about median robustness into an adaptive, bounded, top-rank objective. Our coordinates are heterogeneous tasks, not independent observations of a common location parameter. Benchmark leakage and rephrased contamination can affect measured model performance \\citep{Zhou2023Cheater,Yang2023Rephrased}, but those findings do not calibrate our headroom response. We neither identify contaminated models nor infer that task-specific data necessarily produces the score increments analyzed here. The contribution is an instance-level comparison under stated assumptions, not a claim of first priority over all work on strategic evaluation.\n    46\t\n    47\t\\section{Method}\n    48\t\\subsection{Problem setup and intervention model}\n    49\tLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times d}$ contain higher-is-better task scores, with target row $t$ and original target vector $x=X_t$. For a selected task set $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ and cap $\\rho$, define\n    50\t\\begin{equation}\n    51\t u_j(\\rho)=\\operatorname{round}_{10}\\!\\left(x_j+\\rho(1-x_j)\\right),\\qquad\n    52\t y_j(S,\\rho)=\\begin{cases}u_j(\\rho),&j\\in S,\\\\x_j,&j\\notin S.\\end{cases}\n    53\t \\label{eq:response}\n    54\t\\end{equation}\n    55\tThe evaluated positive caps are $\\rho=1$ and $\\rho=1/2$. Input scores and endpoints are rounded to ten decimal places. A separate no-update control uses zero response. Task selection is the decision variable; the cap is an externally specified condition, not a fitted learning curve. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarizes this distinction.\n    56\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L260]]\n    57\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    58\t\n    59\tWrite $A_r(i;X)$ for row $i$'s aggregate under rule $r$, and $X^{t\\leftarrow y}$ for the modified table. Promotion means co-first within tolerance $\\tol=10^{-10}$:\n    60\t\\begin{equation}\n    61\t A_r(t;X^{t\\leftarrow y})\\geq \\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(i;X^{t\\leftarrow y})-\\tol.\n    62\t \\label{eq:success}\n    63\t\\end{equation}\n    64\tWe minimize the uniform investment\n    65\t\\begin{equation}\n    66\t \\budget_r(t,\\rho)=\\min_S\\{|S|:\\text{Equation~\\eqref{eq:success} holds}\\},\n    67\t \\qquad b_r(t,\\rho)=\\budget_r(t,\\rho)/d.\n    68\t \\label{eq:objective}\n    69\t\\end{equation}\n    70\tIf no subset succeeds, $\\budget_r=\\infty$. An empty successful subset has budget zero; an empty response reporting unattainability does not. Co-first is weaker than strict victory, and the results do not address arbitrary tie-breaking policies.\n    71\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L1]]\n    72\t\n    73\t\\subsection{Design rationale and compared approaches}\n    74\tWe compare arithmetic mean, ordinary median, and pairwise Borda on identical targets and endpoints. The even median averages the two central order statistics. Borda awards one point for a task-level pairwise win and half a point for a tie, then averages across opponents and tasks:\n    75\t\\begin{equation}\n    76\t A_{\\mathrm{B}}(i;X)=\\frac{1}{d(n-1)}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\sum_{h\\ne i}\n    77\t \\left[\\mathbf{1}\\{X_{ij}>X_{hj}\\}+\\tfrac12\\mathbf{1}\\{X_{ij}=X_{hj}\\}\\right].\n    78\t \\label{eq:borda}\n    79\t\\end{equation}\n    80\tChanging the target can lower a rival's Borda total even though the rival's underlying task scores are unchanged.\n    81\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L50]]\n    82\t\n    83\tThe hypothesis is conditional: the ordering of task budgets need not survive a change in cap. This follows mechanistically from distinct sufficient statistics. Mean uses total added score mass; median uses central threshold crossings; Borda uses pairwise crossings against the whole competitor population. None of these mechanisms implies the same ranking of investment costs on every target vector.\n    84\t\n    85\tThere is nevertheless a simple within-rule restriction. Holding the table and target fixed, a larger cap weakly expands the set of successful subsets. Thus, with infinity ordered above finite budgets,\n    86\t\\begin{equation}\n    87\t \\rho_1\\leq\\rho_2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n    88\t \\budget_r(t,\\rho_1)\\geq\\budget_r(t,\\rho_2).\n    89\t \\label{eq:monotone}\n    90\t\\end{equation}\n    91\tThis implication follows from the same target--rival monotonicity used by the endpoint test. It does not order different rules: their budgets may increase by different amounts when the cap shrinks. Observing a reversal between rules therefore does not contradict monotonicity of either rule's own budget. This separation distinguishes the proposed comparison from an assertion that a smaller permitted improvement can make promotion cheaper.\n    92\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L260]]\n    93\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L193]]\n    94\t\n    95\tAllocation methods are compared with no intervention, marginal greedy selection, and a seeded random task order evaluated by successive prefixes. Marginal greedy chooses the task producing the largest current improvement in the target's gap to its strongest rival, with deterministic task-order ties. A random order is drawn for each decision, not optimized over many orders. These are selection policies under the same response model, not alternative training procedures. The original development comparator uses exhaustive allocation on small tables and greedy allocation otherwise; its selection score does not supply a matched final population comparison.\n    96\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py#L59]]\n    97\t% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/baselines]]\n    98\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study]]\n    99\t\n   100\t\\subsection{Scalar allocation certificates}\n   101\tFor mean and median, competitors' aggregates stay fixed. Let $c_r=\\max_{i\\ne t}A_r(i;X)$. Raising selected coordinates to their endpoints cannot reduce any studied aggregate or worsen any target--rival gap. Thus the all-task endpoint vector certifies reachability: if it cannot win, no subset can. Conversely, because selected tasks have unit cost and no penalty for a larger allowed response, endpoint updates suffice for the stated optimization problem.\n   102\t\n   103\tFor the mean, let $g_j=u_j-x_j$, and sort gains decreasingly as $g_{(1)}\\geq\\cdots\\geq g_{(d)}$. The minimum finite budget is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n   104\t\\begin{equation}\n   105\t \\sum_{\\ell=1}^{k} g_{(\\ell)}\\geq d\\bigl(c_{\\mathrm{mean}}-\\tol-\\bar{x}\\bigr).\n   106\t \\label{eq:mean}\n   107\t\\end{equation}\n   108\tNo other $k$ tasks can supply more gain, giving a cardinality certificate rather than just a successful witness.\n   109\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L73]]\n   110\t\n   111\tFor odd $d=2m+1$, median success requires at least $m+1$ coordinates at or above $c_{\\mathrm{median}}-\\tol$. Count those already above that threshold, then count coordinates whose endpoint crosses it. The required number of crossings gives the minimum if enough are available. For even $d=2m$, success depends on the sum of the two central values, not a single threshold. The solver enumerates candidate lower central endpoints and the smallest compatible upper endpoint. It checks the nested requirements of at least $m+1$ values above the lower endpoint and at least $m$ above the upper endpoint, sharing upgrades where possible. Final aggregate checks protect the numerical boundary. The small even-dimensional setting also uses exhaustive subset verification. Appendix~\\ref{sec:scalar} gives the counting construction.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L82]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L93]]\n   114\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L129]]\n   115\t\n   116\t\\subsection{Rival-aware Borda allocation}\n   117\tUse twice-credit integer totals $T_i=2d(n-1)A_{\\mathrm{B}}(i;X)$. Let $\\delta_{ij}\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$ be the increase in the target's twice-credit against rival $i$ on task $j$, and $G_j=\\sum_{i\\ne t}\\delta_{ij}$. Selecting task $j$ increases the target's total by $G_j$ and decreases rival $i$'s total by $\\delta_{ij}$. With binary task indicators $z_j$, the necessary and sufficient constraints are\n   118\t\\begin{equation}\n   119\t \\sum_j (G_j+\\delta_{ij})z_j\\geq D_i,\n   120\t \\quad D_i=\\left\\lceil T_i-T_t-2d(n-1)\\tol\\right\\rceil,\n   121\t \\quad i\\ne t.\n   122\t \\label{eq:cover}\n   123\t\\end{equation}\n   124\tOnly initially positive deficits constrain the solution, since all gap improvements are nonnegative. This formulation accounts for changing rival totals explicitly.\n   125\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L193]]\n   126\t\n   127\tThe supplied selected source builds a feasible gain-ordered prefix, deletes unnecessary tasks, and searches smaller cardinalities using individual-rival bounds and suffix pruning. Search is bounded by a deadline and a node limit; on interruption it returns a verified incumbent, not an optimality assertion. In the recorded original instances, independent reference bounds close and match every finite returned cardinality. That observed agreement is stronger than a feasible-witness check, but it does not establish exactness for arbitrary larger instances or superiority of a particular search component. No controlled component ablation is available.\n   128\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L160]]\n   129\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   130\t\n   131\t\\section{Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol}\n   132\t\\paragraph{Snapshots and preprocessing.}\n   133\tThe study uses HELM's MMLU release v1.13.0 and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot, acquired on 7 November 2026. HELM contributes 57 subject scores; Open LLM contributes six normalized leaderboard columns: IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-PRO. Open LLM scores are divided by 100; its additional raw columns are not treated as independent tasks. The comparison recomputes all three aggregation rules from these component scores. It is not a claim about the live ordering displayed by either website.\n   134\t% @evidence[[data/helm.json#/tasks]]\n   135\t% @evidence[[data/openllm.json#/tasks]]\n   136\t% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/normalization]]\n   137\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   138\t\n   139\tScore content and known model aliases are clustered before a deterministic content-based split separates development from final analysis. Exact duplicates are collapsed; normalization is applied after assignment. The frozen final competitor tables, not just the selected targets, determine every comparison. Known alias matching reduces exact overlap but cannot establish unseen-model-family independence because lineage information is incomplete. Appendix~\\ref{sec:repro} specifies versions, hashes, and the acquisition code.\n   140\t\n   141\t\\paragraph{Targets and matched comparisons.}\n   142\tEach final score table has eight fixed targets, selected at rank-spaced positions among nonwinners under its original arithmetic mean. Target selection is not repeated separately for each rule. Consequently, a target can already be co-first under median or Borda. We retain such zero-budget cases. Each target is assessed under every rule at both positive caps, keeping all other inputs fixed. Target labels are frozen row indices, not model-family categories.\n   143\t% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/target_selection]]\n   144\t% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#/targets]]\n   145\t\n   146\tThe records contain three seeded executions, with seeds 42011, 42013, and 42017, of the same target instances. There are 48 distinct target--rule--cap decisions per score table. Independent validation recomputes aggregate success and checks the reference bounds. The reference uses exhaustive subsets for the six-task table, sorting or threshold counting for scalar rules on HELM, and a time-limited binary optimization reference for HELM Borda. We report a finite optimum only where a valid witness and matching lower and upper bounds are recorded; unresolved optimization would remain a bound interval. Independently certified unattainability is kept separate from missing or failed computation.\n   147\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   148\t% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/baselines]]\n   149\t\n   150\t\\paragraph{Checks and uncertainty.}\n   151\tTask permutations, constant-score tables, and zero-update controls test implementation behavior and are excluded from the empirical target denominator. A recorded reset replay checks a repeated status/effort signature. Repeated seeds do not resample tasks or models. We therefore report deterministic paired differences and observed repeatability, not confidence intervals for an unspecified population. The random comparator's range reflects only its recorded orders. No statistical test is used to turn these fixed-panel counterexamples into a universal rule ranking.\n   152\t\n   153\t\\section{Results: Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets}\n   154\t\\subsection{Within-target reversals}\n   155\tTable~\\ref{tab:budgets} reports every original target. Figure~\\ref{fig:paired} places the mean and median budgets on common normalized axes. Points above the diagonal require more tasks under median; points below it require more under mean. The shaded rails retain unattainable cases rather than assigning them an artificial large finite budget. Multiplicities are explicit, and seeded repeats are not plotted as independent targets.\n   156\t\n   157\tFor HELM target 10, the mean/median budgets are 9/12 tasks at full headroom and 23/12 at half headroom. For Open LLM target 1609, they are 2/3 and 4/3, respectively. Thus the direction of the same-target comparison reverses in both score tables. These examples refute a cap-independent ordering on these instances; they do not estimate how frequently such reversals occur among future models.\n   158\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations]]\n   159\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/4/observations]]\n   160\t\n   161\tThe reversal is interpretable without attributing it to training. Smaller increments reduce the total gain available to the mean, requiring additional coordinates. The median can retain its task count when the necessary endpoint threshold crossings remain possible. HELM targets 37, 18, and 28 exhibit unchanged median counts across the two caps while their mean counts increase. This is a property of their observed score geometry under the stipulated response, not a claim that median is insensitive to cap changes in general. HELM target 40 already has median budget zero at both caps despite being a mean-selected nonwinner.\n   162\t% @evidence[[derived/original_records.json]]\n   163\t\n   164\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   165\t\\centering\\small\n   166\t\\caption{Minimum selected-task counts on all original targets. $\\infty$ means unattainable under the stated cap. Every finite count matches a closed independent reference bound in each recorded seeded execution. Task counts are within-snapshot investments, not calibrated training costs.}\n   167\t\\label{tab:budgets}\n   168\t\\input{tables/budgets.tex}\n   169\t\\end{table}\n   170\t\n   171\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   172\t\\centering\n   173\t\\begin{subfigure}{\\textwidth}\n   174\t\\centering\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/paired_mean_median_budgets.pdf}\n   175\t\\end{subfigure}\n@article{Hendrycks2020MMLU,\n  author = {Dan Hendrycks and Collin Burns and Steven Basart and others},\n  title = {{Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding}},\n  year = {2020},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2009.03300},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2009.03300}\n}\n\n@article{Suzgun2022BBH,\n  author = {Mirac Suzgun and Nathan Scales and Nathanael Schärli and others},\n  title = {{Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them}},\n  year = {2022},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.09261},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.09261}\n}\n\n@article{Liang2022HELM,\n  author = {Percy Liang and Rishi Bommasani and Tony Lee and others},\n  title = {{Holistic Evaluation of Language Models}},\n  year = {2022},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.09110},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.09110}\n}\n\n@article{Dehghani2021Lottery,\n  author = {Mostafa Dehghani and Yi Tay and Alexey A. Gritsenko and others},\n  title = {{The Benchmark Lottery}},\n  year = {2021},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2107.07002},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07002}\n}\n\n@article{Lanctot2023SocialChoice,\n  author = {Marc Lanctot and Kate Larson and Yoram Bachrach and others},\n  title = {{Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory}},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2312.03121},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.03121}\n}\n\n@article{Singh2025Illusion,\n  author = {Shivalika Singh and Yiyang Nan and Alex Wang and others},\n  title = {{The Leaderboard Illusion}},\n  year = {2025},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.20879},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.20879}\n}\n\n@article{Zhou2023Cheater,\n  author = {Kun Zhou and Yutao Zhu and Zhipeng Chen and others},\n  title = {{Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater}},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.01964},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.01964}\n}\n\n@article{Yang2023Rephrased,\n  author = {Shuo Yang and Wei-Lin Chiang and Lianmin Zheng and Joseph E. Gonzalez and Ion Gabriel Stoica},\n  title = {{Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples}},\n  year = {2023},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.04850},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.04850}\n}\n\n@article{Chen2026Strategic,\n  author = {Yatong Chen and Guanhua Zhang and Moritz Hardt},\n  title = {{Leaderboard Incentives: Model Rankings under Strategic Post-Training}},\n  year = {2026},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.08371},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.08371}\n}\n\n@article{Dodge2019Reporting,\n  author = {Jesse Dodge and Suchin Gururangan and Dallas Card and Roy Schwartz and Noah A. Smith},\n  title = {{Show Your Work: Improved Reporting of Experimental Results}},\n  year = {2019},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.03004},\n  url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.03004}\n}\n\n@misc{Gao2023Harness,\n  author = {Lintang Sutawika and Leo Gao and Hailey Schoelkopf and others},\n  title = {{A framework for few-shot language model evaluation}},\n  year = {2021},\n  howpublished = {Zenodo},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.5371628}\n}\n\n@book{Brandt2016Handbook,\n  editor = {Herve J. Moulin},\n  title = {{Handbook of Computational Social Choice}},\n  year = {2016},\n  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1017/cbo9781107446984}\n}\n\n@article{Gibbard1973Manipulation,\n  author = {Allan Gibbard},\n  title = {{Manipulation of Voting Schemes: A General Result}},\n  year = {1973},\n  journal = {Econometrica},\n  volume = {41},\n  pages = {587--587},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.2307/1914083}\n}\n\n@article{Huber1964Robust,\n  author = {Peter J. Huber},\n  title = {{Robust Estimation of a Location Parameter}},\n  year = {1964},\n  journal = {The Annals of Mathematical Statistics},\n  volume = {35},\n  pages = {73--101},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1214/aoms/1177703732}\n}\n\n@article{Hampel1974Influence,\n  author = {Frank R. Hampel},\n  title = {{The Influence Curve and Its Role in Robust Estimation}},\n  year = {1974},\n  journal = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n  volume = {69},\n  pages = {383--383},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1974.10482962}\n}\n\n@article{Faliszewski2009Bribery,\n  author = {Piotr Faliszewski and Edith Hemaspaandra and Lane A. Hemaspaandra},\n  title = {{How Hard Is Bribery in Elections?}},\n  year = {2009},\n  journal = {Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research},\n  volume = {35},\n  pages = {485--532},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1613/jair.2676}\n}\n\n@article{Nemhauser1978Submodular,\n  author = {George L. Nemhauser and Laurence A. Wolsey and Marshall L. Fisher},\n  title = {{An analysis of approximations for maximizing submodular set functions—I}},\n  year = {1978},\n  journal = {Mathematical Programming},\n  volume = {14},\n  pages = {265--294},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1007/bf01588971}\n}\n\n@article{Hoeffding1963Bounds,\n  author = {Wassily Hoeffding},\n  title = {{Probability Inequalities for Sums of Bounded Random Variables}},\n  year = {1963},\n  journal = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n  volume = {58},\n  pages = {13--13},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1963.10500830}\n}\n\n@article{Efron1979Bootstrap,\n  author = {Bradley Efron},\n  title = {{Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife}},\n  year = {1979},\n  journal = {The Annals of Statistics},\n  volume = {7},\n  pages = {1--26},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176344552}\n}\n\n@article{Dwork2001Rank,\n  author = {Cynthia Dwork and Ravi Kumar and Moni Naor and D. Sivakumar},\n  title = {{Rank aggregation methods for the Web}},\n  year = {2001},\n  journal = {International World Wide Web Conference (WWW)},\n  pages = {613--622},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1145/371920.372165}\n}\n\n@article{Rousseeuw1984Median,\n  author = {Peter J. Rousseeuw},\n  title = {{Least Median of Squares Regression}},\n  year = {1984},\n  journal = {Journal of the American Statistical Association},\n  volume = {79},\n  pages = {871--871},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.1984.10477105}\n}\n\n@article{Bertsimas2004Price,\n  author = {Dimitris Bertsimas and Melvyn Sim},\n  title = {{The Price of Robustness}},\n  year = {2004},\n  journal = {Operations Research},\n  volume = {52},\n  pages = {35--53},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.1030.0065}\n}\n\n@article{Bertsimas2011Theory,\n  author = {Dimitris Bertsimas and David B. Brown and Constantine Caramanis},\n  title = {{Theory and Applications of Robust Optimization}},\n  year = {2011},\n  journal = {SIAM Review},\n  volume = {53},\n  pages = {464--501},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1137/080734510}\n}\n\n@article{BenTal1998Robust,\n  author = {Aharon Ben‐Tal and Arkadi S. Nemirovski},\n  title = {{Robust Convex Optimization}},\n  year = {1998},\n  journal = {Mathematics of Operations Research},\n  volume = {23},\n  pages = {769--805},\n  url = {https://doi.org/10.1287/moor.23.4.769}\n}\n\n@article{Demsar2006Comparisons,\n author={Janez Demšar},\n title={{Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets}},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n volume={7},\n pages={1--30},\n year={2006},\n url={https://jmlr.org/papers/v7/demsar06a.html}\n}\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected certified cap-response frontiers for mean versus ordinary median; status=idea_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No executed scientific finding or evaluated refinement justifies changing distilled conclusions.\n  - evidence: Bootstrap correct=true and combined_score=0.7239766081871346 are supplied evidence only. No recent ideas or attempts. The named incumbent source path was absent; implementation defects and criterion weaknesses remain unknown. Task-provided 57-subject HELM and six-task Open LLM snapshots permit exact odd-median counting and small even-median endpoint enumeration. Alternatives considered: broader social-choice/MILP methods add avoidable complexity; training-response calibration lacks observational support. Selected hypothesis: paired rule budgets may reverse with target score geometry and response caps, but reversal is not a required result. Both output JSONs contain the same rubric array; implementation verifications contain one to eight executable snippets. Only one canonical local memory target was created: ideas/01_certified_cap_response_frontiers_for_mean_and_median_leaderboards/memory.md. No task program, evaluator, experiment, or external literature search was run.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T03:44:27.117636+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.7239766081871346, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.7239766081871346, \"correct\": true}\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected budget-specific gain-window and verified anytime Borda search; status=idea_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Source inspection suggests an untested algorithmic refinement, but no evaluated finding establishes a new distilled recommendation.\n  - evidence: Prior attempt equals incumbent combined_score=0.7239766081871346 with correct=true and rubric_overall_pass=false; criterion-level failure cause remains unknown. Permitted source documents anonymous score-upload RPC controls and bounded Borda search with repeated partition bounds and a node cap. No performance bottleneck was measured. Alternatives considered: repeating scalar/provenance frontiers, adding an unconfirmed MILP dependency, and unsupported training-response calibration. Selected implementable NumPy-only Borda refinement. Necessary common-gain floor D-2k follows from per-rival half-win deltas in {0,1,2}; empirical improvement remains a hypothesis. Both bundle rubric arrays were authored identically; implementation checks contain five or six executable snippets. Only one canonical cycle-local memory target was created: ideas/02_gain_window_search_for_rival_aware_borda_investment/memory.md. No candidate, evaluator, experiment, test, optimizer, or external literature search was run.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T03:54:12.473846+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.7239766081871346, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.7239766081871346, \"correct\": true}\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected integer-certified rival-mixture cuts and dual-guided Borda proposals; status=idea_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The proposed coupled-rival refinement is untested and available evaluations do not establish a new evidence-backed distilled recommendation.\n  - evidence: Both recorded attempts equal incumbent combined_score=0.7239766081871346 with correct=true and rubric_overall_pass=false; criterion-level cause remains unknown. Incumbent source paths were unavailable. Named cycle-2 candidate source documents integer Borda covering with independent rival/common-gain bounds; no bottleneck was measured. Alternatives considered: repeating scalar or gain-window work, unconfirmed LP/MILP dependencies, and unsupported causal training-response estimates. Selected NumPy-only coupled-rival certificates. For nonnegative integer w every feasible k-subset requires w dot d <= top_k(w dot A); direction discovery is heuristic, rejection must use strict overflow-safe integer comparisons. Cuts may be redundant or too costly. Both JSON rubric arrays were authored identically with four, five, and six executable snippets. Only ideas/03_integer_rival_mixture_cuts_for_borda_investment/memory.md was created as cycle-local memory; no alternate or staging memory target. File reads and patch writes only; no candidate/evaluator execution, numerical experiment, test, external search, or package installation.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T04:02:14.364350+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.7239766081871346, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.7239766081871346, \"correct\": true}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
