s, correct_programs, evaluated_programs, ideas\n\npaper_strategy.json\nstatus ready\nselected_prompt_family paper_standalone\nrecommended_title Cap-Dependent Task Budgets for Leaderboard Promotion\ncentral_question On fixed public multitask score tables, how does the smallest task subset needed to make a target co-first change with the aggregation rule and the allowed fraction of score headroom?\ncentral_thesis In the evaluated score tables, changing the response cap reverses mean-versus-median investment orderings for some targets and makes other promotions unattainable, so aggregation-rule resistance must be reported conditional on the cap and t\nnarrative_rationale This is a shared static score-space study with condition-specific evidence from HELM MMLU and the six-task Open LLM table, not two chronological experiments. Original final allocations contain independently bounded mean/median reversals in \nprior_paper_story_status rejected\nedit_framing_decision Fresh framing, not an edit pass: no previous_paper manuscript, resolved edit-context files, aggregated directory, or extra_tasks directory was found. The required enum 'rejected' means no inherited manuscript story is adopted, not that an u\nstaged_input_impact list length: 5 first item keys: \nmethod_groups list length: 5 first item keys: ['name', 'role', 'include_reason']\nresults_focus list length: 7 first item keys: \nfigure_plan list length: 3 first item keys: \nfigure_specs list length: 3 first item keys: ['figure_id', 'required', 'purpose', 'figure_type', 'preferred_generator', 'group_by', 'metric', 'split_by', 'preferred_span', 'target_width_fraction', 'layout_rationale', 'allow_progression', 'max_panels', 'max_categories_per_panel', 'max_series_per_panel']\nsupplementary_plan object keys: purpose, planned_subsections\nforbidden_headings list length: 9 first item keys: \nforbidden_phrases list length: 19 first item keys: \nnotes_for_writer list length: 19 first item keys: \nfailure_reason \n\nreferences_provenance.json\nreferences list length: 25 first item keys: ['id', 'citation_key', 'title', 'year', 'source_url', 'metadata_path', 'content_path', 'consultation_scope', 'retrieved_at']\nretrieval_policy Exact task-allowed identifiers, exact pre-2010 titles to repair mismatched identifiers, and exact foundational DOI endpoints only. No citation-graph traversal or related-content expansion.\nexcluded_initial_records Off-topic initial metadata hits were not retained. Unavailable-content classics omitted. Incorrect DOI candidate for bribery was discarded after title mismatch; no substantive claim uses it.\n\nSECTIONS\n19 \\section{Introduction}\n20 Public multitask benchmarks compress a model's performance profile into a leaderboard position. MMLU and BIG-Bench Hard exemplify evaluations with heterogeneous component tasks, while HELM emphasizes transparency and breadth of evaluation \\citep{Hendrycks2020MMLU,Suzgun2022BBH,Liang2022HELM}. Once adoption and recognition depend on position, a developer may have an incentive to concentrate resources on a subset of benchmark tasks. A leaderboard's\n37 \\section{Related Work}\n39 The benchmark lottery shows that task selection can alter comparative conclusions \\citep{Dehghani2021Lottery}. Here the task set stays fixed while the aggregation rule and permitted response change. HELM's multidimensional evaluation and the language-model evaluation harness motivate explicit task definitions and reproducible score processing \\citep{Liang2022HELM,Gao2023Harness}; our computations use released scores rather than rerunning either e\n42 Treating tasks as voters connects model evaluation with social choice \\citep{Lanctot2023SocialChoice,Brandt2016Handbook}; aggregating rankings is also a classical information-retrieval problem \\citep{Dwork2001Rank}. Strategic preference reporting \\citep{Gibbard1973Manipulation} and election bribery \\citep{Faliszewski2009Bribery} establish useful distinctions between aggregation and manipulation. Our intervention changes only a target's numerical \n45 Classical robust estimation distinguishes local influence from resistance under a specified contamination model \\citep{Huber1964Robust,Hampel1974Influence,Rousseeuw1984Median}. These distinctions caution against importing a slogan about median robustness into an adaptive, bounded, top-rank objective. Our coordinates are heterogeneous tasks, not independent observations of a common location parameter. Benchmark leakage and rephrased contamination \n47 \\section{Method}\n48 \\subsection{Problem setup and intervention model}\n73 \\subsection{Design rationale and compared approaches}\n100 \\subsection{Scalar allocation certificates}\n116 \\subsection{Rival-aware Borda allocation}\n131 \\section{Public Score Tables and Comparison Protocol}\n153 \\section{Results: Cap-Dependent Promotion Budgets}\n154 \\subsection{Within-target reversals}\n181 \\subsection{Reachability and Borda}\n198 \\subsection{Allocation checks and comparator interpretation}\n207 \\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n224 \\section{Conclusion}\n234 \\section{Supplementary Information}\n236 \\subsection{Endpoint reduction and scalar certificates}\\label{sec:scalar}\n252 \\subsection{Borda constraints and bounded search}\n260 There is no assertion of a generic greedy approximation guarantee: guarantees for submodular maximization require specific objective and constraint assumptions \\citep{Nemhauser1978Submodular}. Nor is this a robust optimization problem that optimizes against a calibrated uncertainty set. Robust optimization provides a different treatment of uncertain coefficients and conservatism \\citep{BenTal1998Robust,Bertsimas2004Price,Bertsimas2011Theory}. Our\n262 \\subsection{Complete allocation and comparator tables}\n380 \\subsection{Controls, replay, and interpretation of uncertainty}\n385 The three executions repeat a fixed panel, yielding 288 original decision records but only 96 unique target--rule--cap instances. Identical outputs across seeds establish observed repeatability of the allocation, not population uncertainty. The nonrandom target selection and shared competitor tables do not justify treating those records as independent draws for a confidence interval. Classical concentration inequalities and bootstrap inference re\n388 \\subsection{Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map}\\label{sec:repro}\n410 \\subsection{Selection scope and independent final assessment}\n417 \\subsection{LLM Usage}\n\nBIB KEYS ['Hendrycks2020MMLU', 'Suzgun2022BBH', 'Liang2022HELM', 'Dehghani2021Lottery', 'Lanctot2023SocialChoice', 'Singh2025Illusion', 'Zhou2023Cheater', 'Yang2023Rephrased', 'Chen2026Strategic', 'Dodge2019Reporting', 'Gao2023Harness', 'Brandt2016Handbook', 'Gibbard1973Manipulation', 'Huber1964Robust', 'Hampel1974Influence', 'Faliszewski2009Bribery', 'Nemhauser1978Submodular', 'Hoeffding1963Bounds', 'Efron1979Bootstrap', 'Dwork2001Rank', 'Rousseeuw1984Median', 'Bertsimas2004Price', 'Bertsimas2011Theory', 'BenTal1998Robust', 'Demsar2006Comparisons']\nUNUSED []\nUNRESOLVED []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
