re=0.7239766081871346 with correct=true and rubric_overall_pass=false; criterion-level failure cause remains unknown. Permitted source documents anonymous score-upload RPC controls and bounded Borda search with repeated partition bounds and a node cap. No performance bottleneck was measured. Alternatives considered: repeating scalar/provenance frontiers, adding an unconfirmed MILP dependency, and unsupported training-response calibration. Selected implementable NumPy-only Borda refinement. Necessary common-gain floor D-2k follows from per-rival half-win deltas in {0,1,2}; empirical improvement remains a hypothesis. Both bundle rubric arrays were authored identically; implementation checks contain five or six executable snippets. Only one canonical cycle-local memory target was created: ideas/02_gain_window_search_for_rival_aware_borda_investment/memory.md. No candidate, evaluator, experiment, test, optimizer, or external literature search was run.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T03:54:12.473846+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.7239766081871346, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.7239766081871346, \"correct\": true}\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Selected integer-certified rival-mixture cuts and dual-guided Borda proposals; status=idea_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The proposed coupled-rival refinement is untested and available evaluations do not establish a new evidence-backed distilled recommendation.\n  - evidence: Both recorded attempts equal incumbent combined_score=0.7239766081871346 with correct=true and rubric_overall_pass=false; criterion-level cause remains unknown. Incumbent source paths were unavailable. Named cycle-2 candidate source documents integer Borda covering with independent rival/common-gain bounds; no bottleneck was measured. Alternatives considered: repeating scalar or gain-window work, unconfirmed LP/MILP dependencies, and unsupported causal training-response estimates. Selected NumPy-only coupled-rival certificates. For nonnegative integer w every feasible k-subset requires w dot d <= top_k(w dot A); direction discovery is heuristic, rejection must use strict overflow-safe integer comparisons. Cuts may be redundant or too costly. Both JSON rubric arrays were authored identically with four, five, and six executable snippets. Only ideas/03_integer_rival_mixture_cuts_for_borda_investment/memory.md was created as cycle-local memory; no alternate or staging memory target. File reads and patch writes only; no candidate/evaluator execution, numerical experiment, test, external search, or package installation.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T04:02:14.364350+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.7239766081871346, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.7239766081871346, \"correct\": true}\n- next_action: Hand off paper_strategy.md and paper_strategy.json for thesis-first standalone writing. Preserve hypothetical score-response limitations, unreachable cases, zero search delta, original comparator scope, and the exclusion boundary. Do not infer optimizer superiority, causal training cost, generalization, or universal rule robustness.\n\n\n### 2026-11-07T04:20:32.251135+00:00 — paper\nInspected strategy, independent final evidence, scalar/Borda source, comparator and venue constraints. Central thesis retained: cap-conditioned rule ordering, not optimization superiority.\nEvidence: final.qualification.* = completed/qualified/true; study.search_score_delta=0; two public score families; no ablations. Scientific-evidence bundle emitted.\nNext: Build a read-only extractor, references with exact identifiers only, figures and manuscript. Concept rendering remains orchestrator-owned.\n\n### 2026-11-07T04:27:05.813729+00:00 — verify\nExtractor confirms closed independent reference bounds and identical frozen allocations across three seeds. Numerical figures, full allocation tables and pointer-preserving CSV generated.\nEvidence: derived/summary.json, derived/original_records.json, final.evidence.episodes. Bibliography audit rejected unrelated initial hits and corrected three exact DOI mismatches; no citation-graph expansion.\nNext: Finish exact-source content review, draft cap-dependent study, then run all validators.\n\n### 2026-11-07T04:46:19.346255+00:00 — draft\nStandalone manuscript, complete allocation tables, 25 consulted references, reproducibility code, frozen local resource copies and provenance sidecars are written. Repair preserves final qualified outcome independently of tied development selection.\nEvidence: paper.tex; claims_grounding.json; data/*.json hashes match supplied frozen inputs; source_evidence/evaluation.py matches final evaluator digest. No component ablation or training-cost claim. One replay is present, not three.\nNext: Finish strict validation and geometry checks; concept overview remains orchestrator-owned with self-guarding placeholder.\n\n### 2026-11-07T04:51:51.554937+00:00 — final\npaper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs: conditional cap-dependent promotion budgets, no optimizer-improvement or training-cost claim, independent final qualification preserved. Fresh draft passes available strict checks.\nEvidence: Compiled paper.pdf: eight-page anonymous ICLR 2026 body plus references and substantive supplement; 136 mapped claims, 25 consulted references; all 288 recorded witnesses/full-cap failures arithmetically verified; canonical figure schema/density and JSON pointers checked; scientific-evidence report has zero dangling references. Final evaluator source digest matches. Scoped implementation-identity caveat retained.\nNext: Parent must synchronously render the reserved conceptual overview and perform authoritative final geometry/figure validation. Numerical plots and manuscript are ready; no new experiment or evaluator rerun is required.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n        normalized[key] = normalized[key].strip() or None\n\n    metrics = normalized.get(\"metrics\")\n    if metrics is not None and not isinstance(metrics, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"metrics must be an object when present\")\n    rubrics = normalized.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if rubrics is not None and not isinstance(rubrics, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"rubrics must be an array when present\")\n    tags = normalized.get(\"tags\")\n    if tags is not None:\n        if not isinstance(tags, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"tags must be an array when present\")\n        normalized[\"tags\"] = [str(item).strip() for item in tags if str(item).strip()]\n    return normalized\n\nglobal_memory/conclusions.md\nglobal_memory/conclusions_history.jsonl\nglobal_memory/conclusions_other_workers.md\nLeaderboard Incentives:\nModel Rankings under Strategic Post-Training\nYatong Chen*, Guanhua Zhang, Moritz Hardt\nMax Planck Institute for Intelligent Systems, Tübingen and Tübingen AI Center\nAbstract\nInfluential benchmarks incentivize competing model developers to strategically allocate\npost-training resources towards improvements on the leaderboard, a phenomenon dubbed\nbenchmaxxing or training on the test task.\nIn this work, we initiate a principled study of\nthe incentive structure that benchmarks induce. We model benchmarking as a Stackelberg\ngame between a benchmark designer who chooses an evaluation protocol and multiple\nmodel developers who compete simultaneously in a subgame given by the designer’s choice.\nEach competitor has a model of unknown latent quality and can inflate its observed score\nby allocating resources to benchmark-specific improvements. First, we prove that current\nbenchmarks induce games for which no Nash equilibrium between model developers exists.\nThis result suggests one explanation for why current practice leads to misaligned incentives,\nprompting model developers to strategize in opaque ways. However, we prove that under\nmild conditions, a recently proposed evaluation protocol, called tune-before-test, induces a\nbenchmark with a unique Nash equilibrium that ranks models by latent quality. This positive\nresult demonstrates that benchmarks need not set bad incentives, even if current evaluations\ndo.\n1\nIntroduction\nTraditionally, machine learning benchmarks came with a fixed training set, requiring that all\nmodels under comparison train on the same data. The situation has changed with large language\nmodel benchmarks that typically only provide test data, leaving the choice of training data to\nthe model developer. This has raised the concern that model developers can inflate benchmark\nperformance with benchmark-specific tweaks that don’t broadly improve model capabilities. The\nresulting problem, called benchmaxxing or training on the test task, confounds model comparisons\nand may cause misleading leaderboards, as prior work shows [Dominguez-Olmedo et al., 2024,\nSingh et al., 2025]. But the situation need not be a sign of cheating or wrongdoing. Rather,\ninfluential benchmarks incentivize competing model developers to strategically allocate post-\ntraining resources towards improvements on the leaderboard. Although widely recognized, there\nis currently no formal understanding of the incentives that benchmarks set.\nIn this work, we initiate a principled study of the incentive structure that benchmarks induce.\nWe model benchmarking as a Stackelberg game between a benchmark designer and multiple\n*Corresponding author: yatong.chen@tuebingen.mpg.de\n1\narXiv:2603.08371v1  [cs.GT]  9 Mar 2026\n\ncompeting model developers. The designer chooses an evaluation protocol, and the model\ndevelopers compete in a simultaneous-move subgame given by the designer’s choice. Each\ncompetitor has a model of a latent quality — unknown to the benchmark designer — and\ncan inflate the observed model score by allocating additional resources to benchmark-specific\nimprovements at a cost. The designer aims to choose an evaluation protocol so that the resulting\nWe study the complexity of influencing elections through bribery: How computationally complex is it for an external actor to determine whether by paying certain voters to change their preferences a specified candidate can be made the elections winner? We study this problem for election systems as varied as scoring protocols and Dodgson voting, and in a variety of settings regarding homogeneous-vs.-nonhomogeneous electorate bribability, bounded-size-vs.-arbitrary-sized candidate sets, weighted-vs.-unweighted voters, and succinct-vs.-nonsuccinct input specification. We obtain both polynomial-time bribery algorithms and proofs of the intractability of bribery, and indeed our results show that the complexity of bribery is extremely sensitive to the setting. For example, we find settings in which bribery is NP-complete but manipulation (by voters) is in P, and we find settings in which bribing weighted voters is NP-complete but bribing voters with individual bribe thresholds is in P. For the broad class of elections (including plurality, Borda, k-approval, and veto) known as scoring protocols, we prove a dichotomy result for bribery of weighted voters: We find a simple-to-evaluate condition that classifies every case as either NP-complete or in P.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
